00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:31.340
OK, let’s start with some basics.

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:35.360
First, space-time is a giant collection of points called events.

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:38.780
Those events can be anything:
The moment a car begins to move,

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:41.999
the moment it stops, that time you woke up in bed this morning,

00:00:41.999 --> 00:00:45.030
your birth, the Big Bang, or you clicking on this video.

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:48.700
A space-time interval is what you get when you connect two events.

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:52.460
All the events between the front and back of the car, we call its length.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:55.680
All the events between when this car starts and when it stops,

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:57.600
we call its space-time path.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:00.620
How slanted that path is, depends on speed.

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:05.920
Anything with mass: me, the car, a water molecule, the Earth, or a proton;

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:09.440
anything you might call an object
must travel more through time

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:10.780
than it does through space.

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:14.500
The time component is always bigger for the space-time path of an object.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:18.900
Things without mass, like photons of light, travel along diagonal paths,

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:21.540
where the time and space components are the same size.

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:22.840
Wait, the same size?

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:24.720
Yeah, that’s where things get weird.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:28.720
We use something called a “line element”
to measure the size of a space-time path

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.360
and, in flat space-time, it looks like this.

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:35.060
You can see it’s not traditionally flat because of this stupid negative.

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:37.260
Time behaves opposite to space.

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:40.220
For objects like you and me, this isn’t such a big deal.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:43.520
We move more through time, so our paths have size.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:45.020
Light, on the other hand.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:49.480
Light always moves the same through time and space,

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:52.320
which means its time and space perfectly cancel each other.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:55.260
And, since we base all other measurements on space and time,

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:58.460
All measurements in a massless particle’s reference frame are zero.

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:01.460
Photons experience all of time in a single moment

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:03.420
and all of space at a single place.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:07.080
No no, we see it move around at the speed of light, so it must experience both.

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:08.840
You’re not thinking four-dimensionally.

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:12.180
Remember, all motion is relative,
and so is time and space.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:15.320
It’s the universe in which we live,
so you better keep up the pace.

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:18.680
We see light move with great ferocity,
even when that light gleams,

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:22.740
But the closer you get to its velocity,
the smaller space and time seems.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:23.980
