00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:37.300
Kyzer cube mentioned that the speed of light might have been given the symbol “c”

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:40.400
for the Latin word “celeritas” meaning swiftness.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:42.920
There’s actually some historical debate over this.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:47.260
It is true that the “c” for “celeritas” was used extensively in wave equations

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:50.140
during the 1800s for the speed of a wave.

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:50.940
However!

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:54.480
The “c” used for the speed of light didn’t come from a wave equation.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:57.940
It came from a disproven alternative to Maxwell’s electromagnetism,

00:02:57.940 --> 00:02:59.840
where it represented a constant.

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:01.920
History is kind of a blundered mess.

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:06.100
I mentioned that curvature equations in relativity are defined locally

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:10.040
and whoeveriamiam was wonder how local exactly.

00:03:10.049 --> 00:03:12.610
Technically speaking, infinitesimally local!

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:16.549
They really only apply over infinitely small
intervals in space-time,

00:03:16.549 --> 00:03:18.860
which is true of most fundamental physics equations.

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:22.640
If the changes from one event to the next are really small though,

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:27.020
you can get away with applying the word local to whole objects or collection of objects.

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:28.960
But, at that point, you’re just approximating.

